Sec. 71101: The Secretary of Health and Human Services is barred from implementing or enforcing the September 21, 2023 CMS final rule "Streamlining Medicaid; Medicare Savings Program Eligibility Determination and Enrollment" (88 Fed. Reg. 65230) against five specified 42 C.F.R. sections (406.21(c), 435.4, 435.601, 435.911, 435.952) from enactment through September 30, 2034. $1,000,000 is appropriated to the CMS Administrator for FY2026 to carry out this section and Sec. 71102.

Sec. 71102: HHS may not implement or enforce the April 2, 2024 CMS final rule "Medicaid Program; Streamlining the Medicaid, Children's Health Insurance Program, and Basic Health Program Application, Eligibility Determination, Enrollment, and Renewal Processes" (89 Fed. Reg. 22780) against an enumerated list of 42 C.F.R. sections in Parts 431, 435, 447, and 457 from enactment through September 30, 2034.

Sec. 71103: Amends Social Security Act §1902 to add paragraph (a)(88) and new subsections (uu) and (vv), requiring states to regularly obtain enrollee address information (from sources such as USPS returned mail, the National Change of Address Database, and managed care entities) beginning January 1, 2027, and to submit SSNs and other data to a new Secretary-run system, beginning October 1, 2029, to prevent individuals from being simultaneously enrolled in multiple states' Medicaid plans. CHIP is conformed via §2107(e)(1) and §2103(f)(3), and managed care contracts must transmit address information to the state beginning January 1, 2027. $10,000,000 (FY2026) and $20,000,000 (FY2029) are appropriated to CMS.

Sec. 71104: Adds §1902(a)(89) and subsection (ww), requiring the 50 states and D.C., beginning January 1, 2027, to screen the Death Master File at least quarterly to identify deceased Medicaid enrollees, disenroll them, and stop payments on their behalf (except for services furnished before death). If an individual was misidentified as deceased, the state must immediately re-enroll them retroactive to the disenrollment date.

Sec. 71105: Amends §1902(kk)(1) to require that, beginning January 1, 2028, as part of Medicaid provider enrollment, reenrollment, or revalidation—and at least quarterly thereafter—the state check the Death Master File to determine whether the provider or supplier is deceased.

Sec. 71106: Amends Medicaid audit/overpayment rules in §1903(u)(1), allowing the Secretary to use state-conducted audits, capping waivers of erroneous excess payments at the amount exceeding the 0.03 allowable error rate, and adding payments for ineligible individuals or where insufficient information exists to confirm eligibility to the definition of erroneous excess payments. These changes apply beginning with fiscal year 2030.

Sec. 71107: Adds §1902(e)(14)(L), requiring states (the 50 states and D.C.), for redeterminations scheduled on or after the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026, to redetermine eligibility every 6 months for individuals enrolled under §1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII) (expansion adults) or equivalent waiver coverage, exempting certain medically frail individuals. HHS must issue implementation guidance within 180 days, and $75,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71108: Amends §1917(f)(1) to raise the Medicaid home equity limit for long-term care eligibility, allowing states to elect a higher limit (up to $1,000,000) for non-agricultural homes and setting a $1,000,000 cap on the indexed limit, effective January 1, 2028. It also clarifies that the home equity limit must be applied when determining eligibility for nursing facility or other long-term care services.

Sec. 71109: Amends §1903(v) to add paragraph (5), providing that beginning October 1, 2026, no federal Medicaid payment may be made for an individual unless the person is a resident of the 50 states, D.C., or a U.S. territory and is a citizen/national, lawful permanent resident, Cuban/Haitian entrant, or a Compact of Free Association resident. CHIP conforms via §2107(e)(1), and $15,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71110: Adds §1905(kk), providing that beginning October 1, 2026, the FMAP for emergency Medicaid services furnished to certain aliens shall not exceed the state's regular FMAP under §1905(b). $1,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71111: Bars HHS from implementing or enforcing the May 10, 2024 CMS final rule "Medicare and Medicaid Programs; Minimum Staffing Standards for Long-Term Care Facilities and Medicaid Institutional Payment Transparency Reporting" (89 Fed. Reg. 40876) against 42 C.F.R. §§483.5 and 483.35 from enactment through September 30, 2034.

Sec. 71112: Amends §1902(a)(34) and §1905(a) to shorten retroactive Medicaid coverage: expansion adults under §1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII) get coverage from the month before application, while other individuals get coverage from the second month before application (replacing the prior third-month rule); CHIP is conformed via §2102(b)(1)(B)(vi). These changes apply to applications made on or after the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026, and $10,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71113: Prohibits, for the 1-year period beginning on enactment, the use of federal Medicaid direct spending to pay a "prohibited entity"—defined as a §501(c)(3) essential community provider primarily engaged in family planning/reproductive health that provides abortions (except rape, incest, or life-endangering cases) and received more than $800,000 in combined federal and state Medicaid expenditures in FY2023—including payments made directly or through a covered organization such as a managed care entity. $1,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71114: Amends §1905(ii)(3) so that the increased FMAP incentive for states that newly expand Medicaid applies only to states that begin expending amounts for all expansion individuals prior to January 1, 2026.

Sec. 71115: Amends §1903(w)(4) to change the Medicaid provider tax hold-harmless threshold, adding a new subparagraph (D) that sets "applicable percent" limits on net patient revenue by class of items/services—0 percent for non-expansion states without an existing tax, and phased-down caps for expansion states (5.5% in FY2028, 5% in FY2029, 4.5% in FY2030, 4% in FY2031, 3.5% in FY2032 and after), with grandfathering for taxes in effect on enactment. The amendments apply only to the 50 states and D.C., and $20,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71116: Requires the Secretary to revise 42 C.F.R. §438.6(c)(2)(iii) so that, for rating periods beginning on or after enactment, state directed payments are capped at 100% of the specified total published Medicare payment rate for expansion states (those covering all §1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII) individuals) and 110% for other states. Payments with written prior approval before May 1, 2025 (or good-faith efforts), or for rural hospitals approved by enactment, are grandfathered but reduced by 10 percentage points each year beginning with the rating period on or after January 1, 2028, until they reach the applicable cap. $7,000,000 is appropriated for each of FY2026 through FY2033.

Sec. 71117: Amends §1903(w)(3)(E) to add clause (iii) specifying when a Medicaid provider tax is not "generally redistributive" (e.g., lower rates on higher-volume Medicaid taxable units, higher rates on Medicaid than non-Medicaid units, or exclusions with equivalent effect), and adds §1903(w)(7) definitions for "Medicaid taxable unit," "non-Medicaid taxable unit," and "tax rate group." The amendments apply only to the 50 states and D.C. and take effect on enactment, subject to a transition period of up to 3 fiscal years determined by the Secretary.

Sec. 71118: Adds §1115(g), providing that beginning January 1, 2027, the Secretary may not approve (or renew or amend) a Medicaid §1115 demonstration project unless the CMS Chief Actuary certifies it is not expected to increase federal expenditures versus the absence of the project, counting as baseline expenditures those the state could have made through its Medicaid state plan or other authority (including at a different site of service). Savings must be accounted for in subsequent approval periods per a Secretary-specified methodology, and $5,000,000 is appropriated for each of FY2026 and FY2027.

Sec. 71119: Adds §1902(xx), requiring states (50 states and D.C.), beginning the first quarter after December 31, 2026 (or an earlier state-specified date), to condition Medicaid eligibility for "applicable individuals" (expansion adults aged 19–64, excluding specified excluded individuals such as medically frail, caregivers, veterans with total disability, and certain others) on demonstrating community engagement of at least 80 hours per month through work, community service, a work program, half-time education, or a combination, or meeting income thresholds. States must verify compliance ex parte, give a 30-day notice and cure period before denying or disenrolling, conduct outreach, and may not waive these requirements under §1115; a special exemption is available through December 31, 2028 for states making good-faith efforts. The section also bars conflicts of interest for contractors determining compliance, requires an interim final rule by June 1, 2026 (exempt from APA notice-and-comment), and appropriates $100,000,000 for FY2026 formula grants plus $100,000,000 for equal grants to states, and $200,000,000 to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71120: Amends §1916 to add subsection (k), providing that beginning October 1, 2028, "specified individuals" (expansion adults with family income above the poverty line) face no enrollment fee or premium but must be charged state-determined cost sharing greater than $0, subject to limits: no cost sharing for certain excluded services (including primary care, mental health, substance use disorder, FQHC, CCBHC, and rural health clinic services), a per-item cap of $35 (prescription drugs capped per §1916A(c)), and a family aggregate cap of 5% of family income. Providers may require payment as a condition of care, and $15,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2026.

Sec. 71121: Amends §1915(c) to add paragraph (11), allowing the Secretary, beginning July 1, 2028, to approve standalone HCBS waivers for individuals who do not require an institutional level-of-care determination, with an initial 3-year term and 5-year renewals. States must meet requirements including needs-based criteria, no material increase in wait times for existing waiver populations, attestation that per capita expenditures won't exceed institutional care spending, and annual data reporting; no payments may fund third-party practitioner benefits where Medicaid is the primary revenue source. $50,000,000 (FY2026) and $100,000,000 (FY2027) are appropriated to CMS, with the FY2027 funds distributed among states based on their HCBS population share.

Sec. 71201: Adds §1899C to Title XVIII, providing that an individual may be entitled to or enrolled for Medicare only if they are a U.S. citizen/national, lawful permanent resident, Cuban/Haitian entrant, or Compact of Free Association resident. For individuals already entitled or enrolled as of enactment, this applies 18 months after enactment, and the Commissioner of Social Security must complete a review within 1 year and notify affected individuals that their benefits will terminate as of that 18-month date.

Sec. 71202: Amends §1848(t) to extend the temporary 2.5% increase to the Medicare physician fee schedule conversion factor to services furnished on or after January 1, 2026, and before January 1, 2027, and conforms the budget-neutrality adjustment in §1848(c)(2)(B)(iv)(V) to include 2026.

Sec. 71203: Amends §1192(e) of the Social Security Act (Drug Price Negotiation Program orphan drug exclusion) to allow a drug to qualify for the orphan drug exclusion if it is designated for one or more rare diseases or conditions (rather than only one), and adds paragraph (4) treating former orphan drugs by reference to the first day after approval on which the drug is no longer an orphan drug. These amendments apply to initial price applicability years beginning on or after January 1, 2028.

Sec. 71301: Amends §36B(e) of the Internal Revenue Code to allow the premium tax credit only for certain lawfully present aliens ("eligible aliens": lawful permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, and Compact of Free Association residents), with conforming changes to ACA §1411 and §1412 verification and advance determination procedures and to §5000A(d)(3). These amendments (other than the conforming ones) apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026, and the conforming amendments apply to plan years beginning on or after January 1, 2027.

Sec. 71302: Amends §36B(c)(1) by striking subparagraph (B), disallowing the premium tax credit during periods of Medicaid ineligibility due to alien status, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 71303: Amends §36B(c) to add paragraphs (5) and (6), providing that a "coverage month" excludes months before an Exchange verifies the individual's eligibility to enroll and for advance premium tax credit payments (using applicable enrollment information such as household income, eligible alien status, coverage status, and residence), and excludes months for which the Exchange does not meet specified filing requirements under 45 C.F.R. §155.305(f)(4)(iii). It also requires Exchanges to provide a pre-enrollment verification process by August 1 of the prior year, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.

Sec. 71304: Amends §36B(c)(3)(A) to add clause (iii), providing that a "qualified health plan" does not include a plan enrolled in during a special enrollment period based on expected household income relative to the poverty line (as prescribed by HHS) and not in connection with an event or change in circumstances specified by HHS, effective for plan years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 71305: Amends §36B(f)(2) to eliminate the limitation on recapture of advance premium tax credit payments (striking subparagraph (B)), with a conforming amendment to §35(g)(12)(B)(ii), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 71306: Amends §223(c)(2)(E) to permanently provide that a high deductible health plan does not fail HDHP status for lacking a deductible for telehealth and other remote care services, and strikes the corresponding limitation in §223(c)(1)(B)(ii), effective for plan years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 71307: Amends §223(c)(2) to add subparagraph (H), treating bronze and catastrophic plans available as individual coverage through an Exchange under ACA §§1311 or 1321 and described in ACA §1302(d)(1)(A) or (e) as high deductible health plans, effective for months beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 71308: Amends §223(c)(1) to add subparagraph (E), providing that a direct primary care service arrangement (primary care services by primary care practitioners for a fixed periodic fee, capped at $150 per month per individual, or $300 for arrangements covering more than one individual) is not treated as a health plan, and excludes procedures requiring general anesthesia, prescription drugs (other than vaccines), and certain laboratory services from "primary care services." It also amends §223(d)(2)(C) to treat direct primary care fees as medical expenses and adjusts §223(g) inflation indexing, effective for months beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 71401: Amends §2105 of the Social Security Act to add subsection (h), establishing the Rural Health Transformation Program with $10,000,000,000 appropriated for each of FY2026 through FY2030 for allotments to the 50 states. States must submit applications by December 31, 2025 (approved or denied by that date) with a detailed rural health transformation plan and a certification that funds are not used for intergovernmental transfers, certified public expenditures, or non-federal share financing; 50% of each year's funds are allotted equally among approved states and 50% based on Administrator-determined factors (rural population, rural health facilities, hospital situations). Funds must support 3 or more specified health-related activities, no more than 10% may be used for administration, no state matching funds are required, unexpended/unobligated funds as of October 1, 2032 return to the Treasury, and $200,000,000 is appropriated to CMS for FY2025.

Sec. 72001: Increases the public debt limit under 31 U.S.C. §3101(b) by $5,000,000,000,000.

Sec. 73001: Prohibits the use of federal funds, for weeks of unemployment beginning on or after enactment, to pay unemployment compensation to an individual whose base-period wages equal or exceed $1,000,000, or for administrative costs of such payments. Applications must include self-certification of the wage threshold, state agencies must verify wage eligibility using available systems and recover overpayments, and the prohibition covers federal civilian and ex-servicemember unemployment programs, extended benefits, and any other federal unemployment compensation program as determined by the Secretary of Labor. [end of text]


